From 2782fce1873e8dacb5d90eec0f912c5591a12227 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GNB Build <palimpseste@quidam.eu>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:21:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/17] feat(companion): EXCLUSIVE_BLE_WIFI mode,
 reboot-switched BLE/WiFi (V4.3)

New companion mode alongside the existing DUAL_BLE_WIFI (both radios at
once): EXCLUSIVE_BLE_WIFI initializes exactly one of BLE or WiFi per boot,
picked by the persisted wifi_enabled flag, and switches between them via a
full reboot (WIFI or BLUETOOTH home page, press to switch) rather than a
live radio toggle. No new BLEDevice deinit/reinit code path: switching just
flips wifi_enabled, saves prefs, and calls ESP.restart(), landing back on
the same already-exercised single-purpose boot path each time.

Motivation: diagnosed on the Tracker V2 (no PSRAM) that running BLE and
WiFi's own initialization together left WiFi's association without enough
free heap to ever resolve (measured ~33KB free right before WiFi.begin(),
with BLE already up -- no connect, no disconnect event, just silence).
Rather than chase a more ambitious live-coexistence fix without a team or
several boards to validate it over time, this trades true simultaneity for
robustness: never pay for both radios' buffers at once.

Wired through the existing DUAL_BLE_WIFI touch points (wifi_ssid/wifi_pwd/
wifi_enabled custom vars in MyMesh.cpp/NodePrefs.h, the WIFI home page in
UITask.cpp, the wifi_active/wifi_interface/interface_manager plumbing in
main.cpp) by broadening their guards to '#if defined(DUAL_BLE_WIFI) ||
defined(EXCLUSIVE_BLE_WIFI)' wherever the behavior is identical between the
two modes; only main.cpp's setup() bring-up and UITask::toggleWiFi() (plus
the BLUETOOTH page's KEY_ENTER handler, which now mirrors the WIFI page
symmetrically in this mode) actually differ. DUAL_BLE_WIFI's own code paths
are untouched -- confirmed byte-identical firmware.bin before/after on both
existing dual envs (heltec_v4 and heltec_tracker_v2).

New env: heltec_v4_companion_radio_ble_wifi_exclusive. Not needed on this
board (the existing dual env works fine, PSRAM gives it plenty of headroom)
-- offered as a lower-footprint alternative, and to exercise the mechanism
before porting it to boards that actually need it.

Compiled, not yet flashed or tested on real hardware.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
